A||||3||PearlBlueSoul||Modified suspension setups/Stock setups?||||1||
Z||000000||PearlBlueSoul||12-01-2000||10:20 AM||||Hey.  Just been gathering info on Fieros and was curious to know how your suspensions are set up. Is it really double wishbone?  What all can you get for a fiero in the way of suspension stuff?<P>------------------<BR>1987 Toyota MR2 N/A 5 speed<BR>  <A HREF="http://www.atlantamr2s.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.atlantamr2s.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/darionscard</A>  <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard/Thumb.jpg">       <BR><B>Notable mods:</B>  Sus tech springs and sway bars, Toyota strut tower brace, KYB strut inserts, HKS exhaust, NGK plugs/wires, Momo Racer wheels (14x6), Custom intake, Kenwood 12" speaker in front trunk, Jensen amp.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||12-01-2000||10:43 AM||||PBS, First the suspensions between 84-87 and 88 are different. 88's use unequal control arms. (Please note, this is a SIMULAR design to Lotus NOT DESIGNED by Lotus like the rumors say) I have an 86 SE and last winter I installed KYB shocks and struts, KONI swaybays front and rear (84-87 didn't come with rear sways), Eibach lowering springs, and polyurethane bushings. This has made the car very tight. Ride quality has suffered but the cornering has improved 10 fold. As for what's available KYB and KONI make lots of suspension kits. We now have a source making 2" dropped spindles for the front. (Allows us to lower car but not lose ride quality) There was a company making coil-over kits but I don't know if they still are. In stock form the 88 suspension is better than he 84-87 in stock form. <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||12-01-2000||01:10 PM||||Ground Control makes a coil over kit for the rear.. and there are a few people that make drop spindels for the front; 2" Suspensions tech springs, Eibach 3/4", Jamex 1.25", Addco makes sway bars, as does Paeco, Suspension tech and Saner Performance. the obvious (monroe, gabriel, and such for stock struts.) Bilstien (i've got) Koni (I had) KYB (VERY nice) are all the performance units other then Carerra (located here in Atl. will make them but you WILL pay dearly for them) Prothane Full poly bushing kit for under $200. bump steer kits, and tubular a-arms for the rear so they don't fold under high torque applications. and USELESS rear strut tower bar. UNLESS you have a longitudinal mounted motor with the tunk delete option you don't need it.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 12-01-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||PearlBlueSoul||12-01-2000||02:58 PM||||HMM&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>1987 Toyota MR2 N/A 5 speed<BR>  <A HREF="http://www.atlantamr2s.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.atlantamr2s.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/darionscard</A>  <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard/Thumb.jpg">       <BR><B>Notable mods:</B>  Sus tech springs and sway bars, Toyota strut tower brace, KYB strut inserts, HKS exhaust, NGK plugs/wires, Momo Racer wheels (14x6), Custom intake, Kenwood 12" speaker in front trunk, Jensen amp.||||reg||1
